A number of chips the library refuses is not a number it keeps

adl_setNumChips() kept the number it was given before it looked at it: the assignment to m_setup.numChips came first, and the range the number must be in was checked afterwards, on the value already stored. The refusal then left it where it was. The function answers -1 and says why, and the player carries a number of chips it never took -- nought, or a hundred and one, or whatever else was asked for.

What sees it from outside is adl_getNumChips(), which gives back m_setup.numChips: a caller that asks after a refusal is told the number that was refused. The count of four-operator channels is taken from the same field as well, by adlCalculateFourOpChannels() where adl_setNumFourOpsChn() is given -1 for an automatic count, so a count made after a refusal is a count for chips the player does not have. The synth is untouched by all of this, which is why nothing crashes and nothing sounds different; the number is simply not true any more.

ADLplug-Next reads the number the synth really has (adl_getNumChipsObtained()), so its own screens are not the ones that would lie. What brought this out is its fuzz target for what a host does to the synthesiser, which hands the library the numbers a project may hold without holding them to a range first -- that is how a refusal is reached at all, since the plugin settles the count before it sets it. The fault was read rather than crashed into: there is nothing here for a sanitizer to catch.

The number is looked at first now, and kept only if it is one the player may have. What the check reads is the number the caller gave, and in the build for real hardware the number that anything above two chips is mapped to, rather than that number after a cast to unsigned: a negative count is refused by the lower bound where it used to be refused by the upper one after wrapping around it.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is upstream.

diff --git a/src/adlmidi.cpp b/src/adlmidi.cpp
--- a/src/adlmidi.cpp
+++ b/src/adlmidi.cpp
@@ -162,17 +162,21 @@
 #endif
 
 #ifdef ADLMIDI_HW_OPL
-    play->m_setup.numChips = numChips > 2 ? 1 : static_cast<unsigned int>(numChips);
+    const int wanted = numChips > 2 ? 1 : numChips;
 #else
-    play->m_setup.numChips = static_cast<unsigned int>(numChips);
+    const int wanted = numChips;
 #endif
 
-    if((play->m_setup.numChips < 1) || (play->m_setup.numChips > ADL_MAX_CHIPS))
+    /* The number is looked at before it is kept: one that is refused is not a
+       number this player has, and adl_getNumChips() gives back what is kept. */
+    if((wanted < 1) || (wanted > ADL_MAX_CHIPS))
     {
         play->setErrorString("number of chips may only be 1.." ADL_MAX_CHIPS_STR ".\n");
         return -1;
     }
 
+    play->m_setup.numChips = static_cast<unsigned int>(wanted);
+
     int maxFourOps = static_cast<int>(play->m_setup.numChips * 6);
 
     if(play->m_setup.numFourOps > maxFourOps)
